 - Unlike documented in the RFC, every keyword is valid on every frequency. (The
   RFC documents that `byweekno` is only valid on yearly frequencies, for example.)
+- Unlike documented in the RFC, an nth-prefixed `byweekday` value does not
+  combine with plain ones as a union. The RFC reads `BYDAY=+2MO,TU,WE,TH,FR` as
+  the 2nd Monday together with every Tuesday, Wednesday, Thursday and Friday;
+  here a date has to satisfy the plain-weekday set and the nth-weekday position
+  at the same time, which no date can, so the rule yields nothing at all rather
+  than raising an error. This is another case of parity with
+  [python-dateutil](https://labix.org/python-dateutil/), which returns the same
+  empty result. Mix the two forms only through an `RRuleSet`, adding one rule
+  for the nth days and another for the plain ones.
+
+```javascript
+var start = new Date(Date.UTC(2020, 0, 1, 9, 0, 0))
+var yearStart = new Date(Date.UTC(2020, 0, 1))
+var yearEnd = new Date(Date.UTC(2021, 0, 1))
+
+// Yields nothing, rather than the 211 dates the RFC describes.
+new RRule({
+  freq: RRule.YEARLY,
+  dtstart: start,
+  byweekday: [RRule.MO.nth(2), RRule.TU, RRule.WE, RRule.TH, RRule.FR],
+}).between(yearStart, yearEnd).length
+// 0
+
+// Each half on its own behaves as expected.
+new RRule({
+  freq: RRule.YEARLY,
+  dtstart: start,
+  byweekday: RRule.MO.nth(2),
+}).between(yearStart, yearEnd).length
+// 1
+
+new RRule({
+  freq: RRule.YEARLY,
+  dtstart: start,
+  byweekday: [RRule.TU, RRule.WE, RRule.TH, RRule.FR],
+}).between(yearStart, yearEnd).length
+// 210
+```
 
